WireGuard server ready
======================

PMSS also writes a ready-to-import one-device profile to `~/wireguard.txt`.
The manual key workflow below remains useful for extra devices or for users who
prefer generating their private key on the client side.

Server host: %HOSTNAME% (%ENDPOINT%)
Public key:  %PUBLIC_KEY%
Listen port: %LISTEN_PORT%
Internal subnet: 10.90.90.0/24
Recommended MTU: 1420

Client quick start (Linux/macOS)
--------------------------------
  umask 077
  wg genkey | tee private.key | wg pubkey > public.key
  # on the seedbox account, append public.key to:
  #   ~/.wireguard-public-key
  # one key per line; invalid lines are ignored

Client configuration template
-----------------------------
[Interface]
PrivateKey = <client private key>
Address = 10.90.90.X/32
MTU = 1420
DNS = 1.1.1.1

[Peer]
PublicKey = %PUBLIC_KEY%
Endpoint = %ENDPOINT%:%LISTEN_PORT%
AllowedIPs = 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0
PersistentKeepalive = 25

Server behaviour
----------------
- `/etc/wireguard/wg0.conf` is rebuilt automatically from per-user
  `~/.wireguard-public-key` files (one base64 WireGuard public key per line).
- Each valid key is assigned a unique `/32` address inside 10.90.90.0/24 and
  added as a `[Peer]` with `AllowedIPs = 10.90.90.X/32`.
- Invalid or malformed keys are ignored and never reach the server config.
- A cron job refreshes the configuration regularly and restarts `wg-quick@wg0`
  only when the generated config changes.
